 [17592186552428 #edb/ref 1069 "q-3894" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552428 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552428 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is a root of the polynomial ${v}^{4}-4{v}^{3}+7v-28?$\n\nI. $v=-4$\n\nII. $v=4$\n\nIII. $v=2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552428 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4251c35b-5737-5f81-8168-9bb04b9bfceb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552428 #edb/ref 1074 "- Substituting $-4$ for $v$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{4}-4(-4)^{3}+7(-4)-28 & = \\\\ 256+256-28-28 & = \\\\ 456\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $-4$ is substituted for $v.$ Therefore, $-4$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $4$ for $v$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4)^{4}-4(4)^{3}+7(4)-28 & = \\\\ 256-256+28-28 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $4$ is substituted for $v.$ Therefore, $4$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $2$ for $v$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{4}-4(2)^{3}+7(2)-28 & = \\\\ 16-32+14-28 & = \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $2$ is substituted for $v.$ Therefore, $2$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, the root is $v=4,$ so the correct answer is \"II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552435 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552435 #edb/ref 1069 "q-3896" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552435 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552435 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is a root of the polynomial ${u}^{4}-26{u}^{2}+25?$\n\nI. $u=1$\n\nII. $u=5$\n\nIII. $u=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552435 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "492f172a-6ec8-53da-b873-64a863435b9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552435 #edb/ref 1074 "- Substituting $1$ for $u$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{4}-26(1)^{2}+25 & = \\\\ 1-26(1)+25 & = \\\\ 1-26+25 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $1$ is substituted for $u.$ Therefore, $1$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $5$ for $u$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5)^{4}-26(5)^{2}+25 & = \\\\ 625-26(25)+25 & = \\\\ 625-650+25 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $5$ is substituted for $u.$ Therefore, $5$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $3$ for $u$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3)^{4}-26(3)^{2}+25 & = \\\\ 81-26(9)+25 & = \\\\ 81-234+25 & = \\\\ -128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $3$ is substituted for $u.$ Therefore, $3$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, the roots are $u=1$ and $u=5,$ so the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552497 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186552311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552497 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37656" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552497 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552497 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is a root of the polynomial ${x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x+1?$\n\nI. $x=-6$\n\nII. $x=0$\n\nIII. $x=2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552497 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b7f2915e-8d70-5b64-9329-25d718df5c40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552497 #edb/ref 1074 "- Substituting $-6$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6)^{3}-6(-6)^{2}+9(-6)+1 & = \\\\ -216-216-54+1 & = \\\\ -485\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $-6$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $-6$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $0$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{3}-6(0)^{2}+9(0)+1 & = \\\\ 0-0+0+1 & = \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $0$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $0$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $2$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{3}-6(2)^{2}+9(2)+1 & = \\\\ 8-24+18+1 & = \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $2$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $2$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, there are no roots among the given options, so the correct answer is \"None\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552549 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3665dc42-60d5-515f-84ce-302acbb58230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552549 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186552497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552560 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186552549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552560 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "53efb6fc-4790-57dc-b467-c06c75735221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552560 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186552435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552590 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186552560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552590 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a84e39c6-3002-5b16-8eaf-28f7699dae4d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186552590 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186552428 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
